<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Igor Stravinsky</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1882">1882</a>–<a href="/years/1971">1971</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Igor Fyodorovich Stravinsky</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Igor Fyodorovich Stravinsky was a Russian composer and conductor, a French citizen from 1934 and an American one from 1945, and a central figure in modernist music. Born in Oranienbaum near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, he studied under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-rimsky-korsakov">Nikolai Rimsky-Korsakov</a> until 1908. For Sergei Diaghilev's Ballets Russes he wrote The Firebird (1910), Petrushka (1911) and The Rite of Spring (1913), whose premiere caused a near-riot. His career is divided into a Russian period, a neoclassical period (1920–1951) and a serial period (1954–1968), in which he took up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnold-schoenberg">Arnold Schoenberg</a>'s twelve-tone technique. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City in 1971.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/igor-stravinsky.jpg" alt="Igor Stravinsky" width="330" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Grantham Bain Collection · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Igor_Stravinsky_LOC_32392u.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Igor Stravinsky was born on 17 June 1882 in Oranienbaum, now Lomonosov, about 50 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>. His mother, Anna Kirillovna, was an amateur singer and pianist from a family of landowners, and his father, Fyodor Ignatyevich Stravinsky, was a bass at the Mariinsky Theater, descended from Polish landowners. His parents expected him to study law, and he enrolled at the University of Saint Petersburg in 1901, agreeing to attend in exchange for lessons in harmony and counterpoint.</p><p>At university he befriended Vladimir Rimsky-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korsakov-sakhalin">Korsakov</a>, whose father was the composer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-rimsky-korsakov">Nikolai Rimsky-Korsakov</a>. In the summer of 1902 he took a portfolio of pieces to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>. The elder Rimsky-Korsakov was impressed enough to insist that he continue his lessons, advised against entering the Saint Petersburg Conservatory because of its rigorous environment, and agreed to advise him personally on his compositions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The impresario Sergei Diaghilev attended the premiere in February 1909 of Stravinsky's Scherzo fantastique and Feu d'artifice, and commissioned him to orchestrate music by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-chopin">Chopin</a> for the ballet Les Sylphides, staged by the Ballets Russes in April 1909. The three ballets that followed, The Firebird, Petrushka and The Rite of Spring, brought him international fame. Petrushka used Russian folk tunes, two waltzes by Joseph Lanner and a French music hall tune, and contained one of the first prominent uses of bitonality in the &quot;Petrushka chord&quot;.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-bartok">Béla Bartók</a> considered the Russian period to have begun with The Rite of Spring, and Jeremy Noble placed Stravinsky's intensive research into Russian folk material in his years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> from 1914 to 1920. His turn to 18th-century music began with Pulcinella, commissioned by Diaghilev in 1919. In 1939 he went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to deliver the Charles Eliot Norton Lectures at Harvard, published as Poétique musicale, and on 9 March 1940 he married Vera de Bosset. In the 1950s, after meeting Robert Craft, he took up serial techniques, treating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnold-schoenberg">Schoenberg</a>'s twelve-tone method freely and repeating notes, and his orchestration became dark and bass-heavy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Stravinsky died of pulmonary edema on 6 April 1971 in New York City. His last major work, the Requiem Canticles (1966), was performed at his funeral. In his 1936 autobiography, Chronicle of My Life, he wrote that &quot;music is, by its very nature, essentially powerless to express anything at all&quot;. Many supporters were confused by his constant stylistic changes, but later writers recognised his versatile language as important to modernist music, and Philip Glass wrote that no composer of his time was untouched by his work.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Igor Stravinsky” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In music Arnold Schoenberg devised a way of organising sound by twelve-note rows, <b>Igor Stravinsky</b> shocked Paris with The Rite of Spring before turning to a neoclassical style, and Alban Berg and Béla Bartók followed their own paths.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · People</a></li><li><blockquote>In April 1917, after Tsar Nicholas II had abdicated, Sergei Diaghilev asked <b>Igor Stravinsky</b> to orchestrate it for wind instruments to open a concert that would otherwise have begun with the anthem &quot;God Save the Tsar&quot;; Stravinsky worked through the night, helped by Lord Berners and Ernest Ansermet.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-song-of-the-volga-boatmen">The Song of the Volga Boatmen</a></li></ul></section>
